        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61872,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients usually present with symptoms related to an ovarian mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61873,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mixed ovarian carcinomas are rare (&lt;&nbsp;1% of ovarian carcinomas in pathological studies) {26099008}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61874,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is related to the histological types present; most mixed carcinomas are composed of histological types that are associated with endometriosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61875,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There has been only one small study on the pathogenesis of mixed ovarian carcinoma, in which the large majority of mixed tumours showed a common clonal origin {26099008}. Conceivably, a mixed carcinoma could develop through transdifferentiation (plasticity) of one histological type to another or through divergence of two histological types from a common precursor. Endometriosis-associated mixed endometrioid&nbsp;/ clear cell carcinomas are the most common, favouring the latter possibility.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61876,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The macroscopic appearance is related to the histological types present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61877,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>At least two histological types must be clearly recognizable on H&amp;E-stained sections. The different histological components should be confirmed by ancillary testing when appropriate. Any percentage of a second histological type that can be confidently demonstrated is sufficient to label the tumour as mixed. The types present and their percentages should be stated in the diagnostic report.</p>\r\n<p>Mixed carcinoma should be distinguished from pure histological types with ambiguous histomorphology (i.e. features intermediate between two histological types) and/or heterogeneous histomorphology (morphological mimicry) {26099008}. Mixed carcinomas should also be distinguished from independent collision tumours, which do not share molecular alterations; these are usually spatially distinct.</p>\r\n<p>The most common mixed tumour is endometrioid and clear cell {26099008}. A combination of napsin&nbsp;A (expressed in the clear cell component) and PR (expressed in the endometrioid component) helps to discriminate between the two components {26974996}. Mixed endometrioid and clear cell carcinomas often exhibit mismatch repair deficiency. Of note, mismatch repair&ndash;deficient endometrioid carcinomas often exhibit morphological heterogeneity, which can be misinterpreted as mixed endometrioid and clear cell carcinoma {26799366}.</p>\r\n<p>Dedifferentiated carcinoma is a special type of mixed carcinoma and is considered separately (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/34/55\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Undifferentiated and dedifferentiated carcinomas of the ovary</em>). Mixed neuroendocrine neoplasms (NENs) are also considered separately (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/34/409\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Carcinoma admixed with neuroendocrine carcinoma</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-09T15:10:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61878,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The cytology is related to the histological types present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61879,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61880,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> the presence of two (or more) ovarian carcinoma histological types, with the components showing distinct and unequivocal differences by histomorphology.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> differences between the two components based on ancillary testing.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61881,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Mixed carcinoma is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:11:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61882,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Prognosis and prediction are dependent on the histological types present.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]